A||||10||frontal lobe||Hail the Fiero!||||8||
Z||000000||frontal lobe||05-18-2000||04:20 PM||||I've had the Fiero a grand total of about 3 months now and IT'S BEEN HAILED ON TWICE NOW!!!. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  Not little baby hail either but big honking ones.  Well, OK, marble size, but still...   Just had to let someone who would care know.  (My wife usually fills that role, but won't in this case).  I feel better now.  Thanks for listening.||||reg||8
Z||000001||deceler8||05-18-2000||04:41 PM||||Any damage...I've been wondering if hail would damage any of the panels on a Fiero...we occasionally get the golfball/baseball-sized stuff here and it can really do a number on cars.||||reg||1
Z||000002||frontal lobe||05-18-2000||04:50 PM||||The panels and paint survived the first vicious onslaught unscathed.  I'm at work now and haven't been out to the car since today's attack.  I'm afraid to go look.  I'll let you know later.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Homicidal||05-18-2000||05:20 PM||||Two words man:<P>CAR COVER.<P>Simply as that.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fformula88||05-18-2000||05:24 PM||||The Fiero will handle hail a lot better than its steel counterparts.  But I'd take every precaution to avoid it if possible.  You'd hate to get a cracked panel, or paint.  You wanna keep that Fiero looking nice!||||reg||6
Z||000005||stimpy||05-18-2000||07:25 PM||||Unless Sealy Posturepedic started making car covers, I don't think so Homicidal!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Here in Oklahoma you can get some nice grapefruit sized wind driven hail... the kind that can kill ya! I hope I never see it in my Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000006||SCCA FIERO||05-18-2000||07:53 PM||||My old Fiero got hailed on once... just cracked a sail panel.||||reg||1
Z||000007||frontal lobe||05-19-2000||12:20 AM||||The Fiero survived without any damage!!  That's one tough little car.  The hail was so bad that in some areas it looked like it had snowed!  Stripped a lot of my plants and trees of leaves.  Grrrr.<P>Car cover is good advice at home, but is it really practical to take it with me to work and put it on while at work and then come out of work and take it off?  Because hail is pretty unpredictable and wasn't in the forecast.  Just being honest, but I am WAY too lazy to do that.<P>Off the fiero subject, but about 10 years ago I got caught in a SEVERE hailstorm that actually stopped traffic completely on a 6 lane highway.  When it was done, my car had as many dimples as a golf ball.  $6000 worth of body damage!!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Raydar||05-19-2000||10:32 AM||||Doc, Glad you came through, unscathed. Please send some of that rain to GA. (You can keep the hail, thank you very much.) It's so hot and dry here that the pine trees are starting to follow the dogs around. Not pretty.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||05-19-2000||10:58 AM||||Two of my Fieros were sitting outside during a hail storm that caused over $2,000 worth of damage to my Suburban, it even broke the windshield. My insurance adjustor and I could not find one single thing wrong with either Fiero. Plastic cars rule.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 05-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||deceler8||05-19-2000||01:58 PM||||I just have to think that keeping a car waxed can minimize damage in a storm...if water beads, hail might deflect better and be less likely to cause damage.<P>But what the hell do I know.||||reg||1
